      "filename": "Kīlauea Visitor Center, Hawaiʻi Volcanoes National Park, Hawaii (4529201582).jpg",
      "title": "File:Kīlauea Visitor Center, Hawaiʻi Volcanoes National Park, Hawaii (4529201582).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/K%C4%ABlauea_Visitor_Center%2C_Hawai%CA%BBi_Volcanoes_National_Park%2C_Hawaii_%284529201582%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C4%ABlauea_Visitor_Center,_Hawai%CA%BBi_Volcanoes_National_Park,_Hawaii_(4529201582).jpg",
      "width": 2816,
      "height": 2112,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Ken Lund from Reno, Nevada, USA",
      "description": "Hawaiʻi Volcanoes National Park, established in 1916, is a United States National Park located in the U.S. State of Hawaiʻi on the island of Hawaiʻi. It encompasses two active volcanoes: Kīlauea, one of the world's most active volcanoes, and Mauna Loa, the world's most massive volcano. The park gives scientists insight into the birth of the Hawaiian Islands and ongoing studies into the processes of vulcanism. For visitors, the park offers dramatic volcanic landscapes as well as glimpses of rare flora and fauna.\nIn recognition of its outstanding natural values, Hawaiʻi Volcanoes National Park was designated as an International Biosphere Reserve in 1980 and a World Heritage Site in 1987. In 2000 the name was changed by the Hawaiian National Park Language Correction Act of 2000 observing the Hawaiian spelling.\nThe park includes 323,431 acres (505.36 sq mi; 1,308.88 km2) of land. Over half of the park is designated the Hawaii Volcanoes Wilderness area and provides unusual hiking and camping opportunities. The park encompasses diverse environments that range from sea level to the summit of the Earth's most massive volcano, Mauna Loa at 13,677 feet (4,169 m). Climates range from lush tropical rain forests, to the arid and barren Kaʻū Desert.\nActive eruptive sites include the main caldera of Kīlauea and a more active but remote vent called Puʻu ʻŌʻō.\nThe main entrance to the park is from the Hawaii Belt Road. The Chain of Craters Road, as the name implies, leads past several craters from historic eruptions to the coast. It used to continue to another entrance to the park near the town of Kalapana, but that portion is now covered by a lava flow.\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hawaii_Volcanoes_National_Park\n\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Text_of_Creative_Commons_...",
      "dateCreated": "2010-04-15 14:55",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "chain-of-craters-road-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "8e3w",
